1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:01:41,730
 Let's say, let's say, so much to learn.

2
00:01:41,730 --> 00:01:46,460
 Today, I'm going to tell you the story in the world should I tell you about.

3
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:48,000
 Tastes as lousy.

4
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:48,620
 Sure is.

5
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:51,490
 You don't know about all the discoveries the way Maestro does,

6
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:53,250
 all the great scientists and inventors.

7
00:01:53,390 --> 00:01:53,850
 What was that?

8
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:36,440
 Changed our lives for thousands of years.

9
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:37,090
 You mean it?

10
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:38,660
 Of years we've had cars?

11
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:40,190
 No, no, of course not.

12
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:41,210
 I mean the wheel.

13
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:45,000
 We can't invent the car if we haven't invented the wheel first, right?

14
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:49,610
 Then that goes back 5,000 years to Samaria in the Middle East.

15
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:39,870
 This is off a beam, and then they attack to a board.

16
00:03:39,870 --> 00:03:42,650
 And one donkey power, it goes fast.

17
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:52,690
 Interesting. We shall try it. If it works, you will be rewarded. If not, you need slices of a beam.

18
00:03:52,970 --> 00:03:53,730
 Oh, yes, sir.

19
00:03:54,550 --> 00:04:45,930
 We're used with horses, and even zebras, but even back then, people dreamed of having a cart with a motor, even Leonardo da Vinci.

20
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:49,090
 Rings could make the wheels turn, maybe.

21
00:04:49,970 --> 00:04:51,110
 What if I, um...

22
00:04:51,110 --> 00:04:54,370
 Yes, but Leonardo would abandon this project as he had done with so many others.

23
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:58,540
 The first true automotive vehicle would be Cugno's steam chariot. It didn't turn out too well.

24
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:02,210
 A few years later, a Scotsman, William Murdoch, built a steam car.

25
00:05:02,490 --> 00:05:04,670
 All's after me.

26
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:08,710
 The Frenchman André Lenoir will invent an internal combustion engine.

27
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:16,500
 In fact, it wasn't to prove very useful.

28
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:18,750
 Then a German, Nicholas Otto.

29
00:05:18,750 --> 00:05:22,560
 Ja, Helen, here's engines that will revolutionize the world.

30
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:24,210
 You think so, Herr Otto?

31
00:05:24,210 --> 00:05:27,130
 I am certain it is the first explosion engine.

32
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:28,810
 Explosion?

33
00:05:28,810 --> 00:05:30,300
 Yes, my Herr, explosion.

34
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:31,450
 You want to see?

35
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:40,410
 Oh, you see an electric spark is causing explosions of the fuel air mixture internally.

36
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:51,200
 This would be the first internal combustion engine, so-called because regular explosions of the air and fuel mixture supplied the power to push the pistons.

37
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:59,070
 The first motorized tricycle was made by the German Carl Benz, and the first true automobile was made by another German, Gottlieb Daimler.

38
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:13,610
 This was to be the start of an extraordinary era. Everywhere they began constructing motorcars, each one was more spectacular than the last!

39
00:06:13,610 --> 00:06:24,160
 As you just imagine, in France, the Peugeot Quadridge, Pont-Ain, Le Vasseur, and De La Haye.

40
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:27,550
 In Germany, the Benz Milord.

41
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:29,180
 Back in France, the Renault.

42
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:31,260
 And the de Dion Bouton.

43
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:34,250
 In Italy, the Fiat, three and a half horsepower.

44
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:36,930
 In England, the Wolseley and the Rolls-Royce Silver Ghost.

45
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:39,650
 I'd like to buy 'em all up.

46
00:06:39,650 --> 00:06:44,160
 These magnificent old cars, they remind me.

47
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:46,160
 It was an epic you know nothing about.

48
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,390
 You weren't even born.

49
00:06:47,390 --> 00:06:50,660
 Neither was I, anyway, I don't think I was.

50
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:54,460
 Naturally, they cost a great deal, and intended only for the privileged.

51
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:57,550
 All that was going to change on account of one man,

52
00:06:57,550 --> 00:07:01,050
 A farmer's son in the United States and his name was Henry Ford.

53
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:36,080
 There won't be any horse-drawn carriages in the cities.

54
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:38,410
 The future is in vehicles with engines.

55
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:40,670
 Nothing will ever replace the horse, Henry.

56
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,460
 Steam engine isn't practical.

57
00:07:42,690 --> 00:07:43,230
 Look at that.

58
00:07:56,170 --> 00:08:11,580
 City, good horse.

59
00:08:12,130 --> 00:08:15,380
 There could be other ways besides steam to power a vehicle.

60
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:17,010
 Henry?

61
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:17,630
 Yes, Dad?

62
00:08:18,710 --> 00:08:20,080
 Troy, you want to come?

63
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:21,820
 Sure, you bet.

64
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:36,260
 She'll find it very interesting.

65
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:36,890
 Yes, sir.

66
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:44,090
 Whistle out the steam engine.

67
00:08:44,110 --> 00:08:44,950
 And it doesn't work?

68
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,140
 No, it doesn't work.

69
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:48,100
 Otherwise, you think I'd blow my lungs out?

70
00:08:49,250 --> 00:08:50,720
 You suppose I might look?

71
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:52,080
 Yeah, one more know-it-all.

72
00:08:59,710 --> 00:09:02,250
 Here, lad, talent for mechanical things.

73
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:03,350
 Well, I try.

74
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:05,730
 Let me show you over here. Come, it's interesting.

75
00:09:06,150 --> 00:09:09,560
 A steam engine. A customer brought it in.

76
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:12,130
 I can't figure out why it won't run. You take a look at it.

77
00:09:12,310 --> 00:09:13,140
 Sure, I'd be glad to.

78
00:09:19,570 --> 00:09:37,180
 To build a motor car with an internal combustion engine.

79
00:09:37,830 --> 00:09:40,570
 Yeah, I've heard about such things. It appears they might work.

80
00:09:40,770 --> 00:09:43,320
 No doubt about it. I made a drawing. Here, look.

81
00:09:44,130 --> 00:09:47,680
 William, I need Henry to work on your farm.

82
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:48,520
 You bet I do.

83
00:09:48,810 --> 00:09:52,950
 I just got a large tract of land so that Henry would have his farm right next to mine.

84
00:09:53,110 --> 00:09:58,040
 i'll tell you no doubt about it he's a born mechanic if he wants i'd be glad to hire him

85
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:03,360
 yes it's what i've always wanted i don't see any future in motorcars but if that's what you really

86
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:12,810
 want oh it is dad believe me say uh mr flower yes henry what is it to make my car it could be

87
00:10:12,810 --> 00:10:17,000
 useful to learn about electricity i've been offered a position at the edison electricity

88
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,990
 generator if you wouldn't mind absolutely henry you go ahead and if you ever want to come back

89
00:10:21,990 --> 00:10:23,450
 Here, you know you've always got a job.

90
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:30,850
 A serious power failure over at Begley's.

91
00:10:30,950 --> 00:10:31,990
 Go and fix it.

92
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:44,000
 Failure at Mr. Hicks.

93
00:10:46,250 --> 00:10:49,190
 Today, they're all waiting.

94
00:10:49,210 --> 00:10:50,570
 You're absent-minded, Henry.

95
00:10:50,970 --> 00:10:52,660
 Guess I'm always thinking of my work.

96
00:10:52,990 --> 00:10:53,830
 Go on, my lad.

97
00:10:53,890 --> 00:10:55,650
 Your own wedding's something not to miss.

98
00:10:56,080 --> 00:10:57,580
 I'll go and see about the power failure.

99
00:11:00,100 --> 00:11:29,660
 Get your help.

100
00:11:32,410 --> 00:11:35,310
 Tell you, when I tell you to, you let the gas in real slow.

101
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:38,680
 Start the drops now.

102
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:44,430
 Stop.

103
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:48,340
 Now I know that I can build a two-cylinder engine as well as a car.

104
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:49,840
 Henry, you're a genius.

105
00:11:50,570 --> 00:11:51,770
 Don't talk about it.

106
00:11:52,030 --> 00:11:52,700
 Not a word.

107
00:11:53,170 --> 00:11:54,620
 Be sure that I'm right.

108
00:11:55,690 --> 00:11:58,320
 Hey, dear.

109
00:11:59,450 --> 00:12:02,330
 It's right away.

110
00:12:03,290 --> 00:12:07,050
 I missed it.

111
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:12,770
 I'll give her a test.

112
00:12:14,810 --> 00:12:16,570
 Too small.

113
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:18,050
 This won't do.

114
00:12:18,130 --> 00:12:19,210
 I have to get her out.

115
00:13:01,990 --> 00:13:20,590
 Horses.

116
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:22,430
 One thing they don't dirty the streets.

117
00:13:22,570 --> 00:13:23,070
 Oh, automobile.

118
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:24,520
 No, huh?

119
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:30,410
 Mr. Ford, these gentlemen and I are planning to set up an automobile factory.

120
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:31,610
 Would you like to join us?

121
00:13:31,610 --> 00:13:32,410
 Yes, I would.

122
00:13:36,830 --> 00:13:42,140
 These aren't powerful enough.

123
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:46,610
 Ford, your automobile is still not ready?

124
00:13:46,920 --> 00:13:47,700
 No, it's not.

125
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:51,180
 I have to run additional tests to be certain that the automobile is quite safe.

126
00:13:51,180 --> 00:13:54,980
 You have already wasted three years, and we have spent a great deal of money.

127
00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:56,700
 You are fired, Mr. Ford.

128
00:13:56,970 --> 00:13:58,830
 I believe you're going to regret this.

129
00:13:59,590 --> 00:14:01,090
 Clara, we have almost no money.

130
00:14:01,570 --> 00:14:04,130
 Then we'll leave here and live with Dad.

131
00:14:04,380 --> 00:14:06,630
 I'll succeed, Clara, I promise.

132
00:14:06,730 --> 00:14:08,310
 I'm nearly finished with my racing car.

133
00:14:08,470 --> 00:14:09,960
 I know you'll succeed, Henry.

134
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:14,860
 Come and see you in a race between the steam-driven cars and gas stations.

135
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:15,780
 It's a great race.

136
00:14:20,100 --> 00:14:21,380
 There they go, they're up there.

137
00:14:21,380 --> 00:15:08,310
 One is going to start.

138
00:16:01,070 --> 00:16:58,330
 Mr. Ford, be a shareholder.

139
00:17:04,240 --> 00:17:10,290
 In order that, a hundred years ago, America already had superb Cadillacs, Oldsmobiles,

140
00:17:10,610 --> 00:17:13,460
 and in Europe, they were also starting to make fine cars.

141
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:23,620
 Mercedes, De Diambouton, wanted to manufacture cars that ordinary people might own,

142
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:27,850
 but the profit seekers were on the lookout for their profits.

143
00:17:29,770 --> 00:17:32,960
 Taken out of the internal combustion engine, and from now on,

144
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:37,380
 anybody who tries to build a car anywhere in the United States pays me a fat fee.

145
00:17:37,380 --> 00:17:39,200
 It should amount to quite a lot for us.

146
00:17:45,350 --> 00:17:49,440
 Mr. Ford, it's a catastrophe!

147
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:53,020
 A certain Mr. Seldon has patented the internal combustion engine.

148
00:17:53,360 --> 00:17:56,220
 I have to pay a lot now. It's going to ruin us.

149
00:17:57,540 --> 00:17:58,020
 Ridiculous.

150
00:17:58,340 --> 00:18:00,460
 It was a Frenchman, René Lenoir,

151
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:03,740
 who invented the original internal combustion engine more than 40 years ago.

152
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:07,280
 And then his engine was improved 20 years later by a German named Otto.

153
00:18:07,690 --> 00:18:09,090
 The Seldon has no right.

154
00:18:09,770 --> 00:18:12,030
 There was a real competition, and it was fierce.

155
00:18:12,570 --> 00:18:14,310
 There were over 150 car builders.

156
00:18:14,470 --> 00:18:16,950
 A huge automobile show was held in New York City.

157
00:18:18,670 --> 00:18:21,510
 Where can I find the exhibition stand for Ford?

158
00:18:21,990 --> 00:18:24,160
 You're not a member of our association.

159
00:18:24,550 --> 00:18:25,590
 No stand for you.

160
00:18:25,990 --> 00:18:27,650
 You have no right to refuse us.

161
00:18:27,710 --> 00:18:28,890
 I have a card order here.

162
00:18:28,950 --> 00:18:29,970
 Yeah, all right, come with me.

163
00:18:30,050 --> 00:18:31,710
 I'll show you just where you can put it.

164
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:41,170
 Right here.

165
00:18:42,090 --> 00:18:44,470
 One or two more and she's all tuned up.

166
00:18:44,510 --> 00:18:46,410
 They put us in the cellar, nobody will come.

167
00:18:46,490 --> 00:18:47,810
 Oh, yes, they will, Kulik,

168
00:18:47,810 --> 00:18:51,310
 because a Ford car is going to break the world speed record.

169
00:18:53,130 --> 00:18:59,030
 I'll drive.

170
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:00,750
 You're going to hold on to the chassis.

171
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:04,820
 You'll have to inject gasoline rapidly under high pressure to increase the power.

172
00:20:25,210 --> 00:20:27,180
 Manufactured assembly line.

173
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:30,570
 The Model T became one of the most popular cars ever built.

174
00:20:30,650 --> 00:20:31,910
 The biggest seller in history.

175
00:20:32,340 --> 00:20:35,080
 They were sold in their millions over a 20-year period.

176
00:20:36,260 --> 00:20:39,140
 Here, Frank, we're going to enter another race.

177
00:20:39,680 --> 00:20:43,040
 It'll be the longest ever run in the United States, Atlantic to Pacific.

178
00:20:43,620 --> 00:20:44,880
 We'll race two cars.

179
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:48,540
 You will drive one of them, but I won't be there because I have to go and testify in court.

180
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:49,440
 Okay, Henry.

181
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:51,460
 Mr. Ford, you may proceed.

182
00:20:51,460 --> 00:20:55,760
 Your Honor, the internal combustion engine was invented by a Frenchman half a century ago.

183
00:20:56,180 --> 00:20:58,520
 Essentially, steam was not used to drive the pistons.

184
00:20:58,620 --> 00:21:00,080
 It was hot, expanding gases.

185
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:02,300
 Mr. Selman didn't invent anything.

186
00:21:15,740 --> 00:21:47,810
 Twin Falls?

187
00:21:48,160 --> 00:23:16,730
 I'll sell 250,000 cars in one year.

188
00:23:18,050 --> 00:23:21,880
 Two years, we've cut the price in half from $1,000 to $500.

189
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:25,200
 And I propose right now to double the salaries of all our workers.

190
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:26,600
 Out of the question.

191
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:27,740
 This is a business.

192
00:23:27,980 --> 00:23:29,500
 We're not a charitable institution.

193
00:23:29,820 --> 00:23:32,820
 It's the stockholders, not the workers, who are entitled to it, Henry.

194
00:23:33,140 --> 00:23:37,060
 We already have a lot more money than any of us needs, Mr. Dodge.

195
00:23:37,060 --> 00:23:39,300
 In a real sense, it was a revolution.

196
00:23:39,740 --> 00:23:40,300
 The workers.

197
00:23:40,810 --> 00:23:44,370
 And not only the bosses were to benefit from industrialization.

198
00:23:44,770 --> 00:23:48,270
 Ford sold like hotcakes, and they were used for all sorts of things.

199
00:23:49,290 --> 00:23:50,330
 Hey-oh-oh!

200
00:24:00,210 --> 00:24:17,720
 That's right, Frank. We've sold 10 million of our Model T's.

201
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:21,030
 Guess I won my bet, because almost anybody can buy a Ford.

202
00:24:21,230 --> 00:24:23,490
 I think it was the golden era of the automobile.

203
00:24:23,930 --> 00:24:26,670
 The roaring 20s. Just look at these beauties.

204
00:24:27,110 --> 00:24:30,890
 Mercedes-Benz, Duesenberg, Hotchkiss, Rolls-Royce, Lancia, Hispano.

205
00:24:30,890 --> 00:24:36,670
 We're all pretty slow.

206
00:24:36,750 --> 00:24:38,270
 Pretty slow? Well, you just listen.

207
00:24:38,390 --> 00:24:43,290
 In 1927, the Golden Arrow attained 370 kilometers an hour.

208
00:24:43,290 --> 00:24:52,110
 In 1970, the blue flame reached a thousand per hour,

209
00:24:52,110 --> 00:24:56,790
 at over a thousand kilometers an hour.

210
00:24:56,790 --> 00:25:00,400
 And now you don't see a car go that fast every day.

211
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:03,340
 And if the motor cars become a part of our daily lives,

212
00:25:03,340 --> 00:25:04,840
 we have Henry Ford to thank.

213
00:25:04,840 --> 00:25:07,840
 True, he also gave us traffic jams.

214
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:10,990
 But I guess, in its way, that too is progress.

215
00:25:10,990 --> 00:25:24,930
 Démons et vagabonds tout ailleurs

216
00:25:24,930 --> 00:25:28,890
 Si que l'homme décolle, il va quitter le sol,

217
00:25:28,890 --> 00:25:33,820
 mais par c'est qu'il s'en va

218
00:25:33,820 --> 00:25:34,530
 Maman

